================================================================================
thinking-orbs — https://github.com/Jakubantalik/thinking-orbs
================================================================================

Sources/PeeksyCore/Orb/OrbDot.swift, OrbMath.swift, OrbMode.swift,
OrbitsProfile.swift, OrbitsMode.swift and GlobeMode.swift are a port of
thinking-orbs' src/engine/core.ts, src/engine/orbits.ts and the drawGlobe half
of src/engine/lattice.ts, with the tuning from src/engine/profiles.ts and
src/presets.ts. The rendering in Sources/Peeksy/Views/OrbCanvas.swift
follows that project's `paint`.

Upstream is TypeScript rendering to an HTML5 canvas; this is a Swift port to
SwiftUI's Canvas, not a bundled copy. Two of the six modes are ported —
`orbits` (upstream's "working") and `globe` (upstream's "searching"). The
`rubik` and `wave` modes that share lattice.ts, the ribbon and morph modes,
and the `fibDir` helper that serves them, are not. `globe` is the one that
ships; `orbits` remains reachable from --orb-lab as the comparison that
justifies the choice.

Five deliberate deviations, all documented at their call sites:

  1. Angular rates are quantised onto a 1/8 rad lattice. Upstream: orbits yaw
     0.12 and per-orbit 0.25 + 0.55h; globe tilt 0.35 and scan 5.702.
     Upstream is driven by performance.now(), a clock that only counts up and
     so never has to close a loop; this port is driven by a repeatForever
     phase ramp that must. The quantisation gives every mode a period of
     exactly 16π and makes the wrap seamless.
  2. Colour. Upstream is strictly monochrome and carries depth in a grey
     level; here the ink value modulates a per-state tint instead, because in
     this app colour is what carries state.
  3. The six-mode preset machinery is replaced by one baked profile per mode.
     The derivation from upstream's base profile and `size: 20` preset is
     written out in OrbitsProfile.swift and GlobeMode.swift.
  4. `Dot.white` is renamed to `OrbDot.ink`.
  5. Dots are sorted by a total order (z, then x, then y) rather than by depth
     alone. Upstream can rely on a stable Array.prototype.sort; Swift's sort is
     not stable, and a globe frame is 54 dots with only 37 distinct depths.

